Get Started Today

</a></p>

<h2><b>DIY, Call a Cleaning Pro, or Call a Design Team?</b></h2>
<table><tbody><tr><td><b>Situation</b></td><td><b>What to Do</b></td></tr><tr><td>Light dirt, leaves, surface dust</td><td>DIY sweeping/rinsing is fine</td></tr><tr><td>Mold, mildew, algae staining</td><td>A cleaning or pressure washing service can typically handle this</td></tr><tr><td>Salt residue, efflorescence</td><td>A cleaning pro with the right technique for your material</td></tr><tr><td>Cracked or spalling concrete</td><td>Worth a professional assessment before more cleaning is done</td></tr><tr><td>Sunken, shifting, or heaving pavers</td><td>Usually a base or drainage issue, this is where a design/install team comes in</td></tr><tr><td>Patio or driveway just looks dated or undersized</td><td>Not a cleaning problem at all, this is a redesign opportunity</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Pressure washing pavers and natural stone is also not a great DIY project. Too much pressure or the wrong nozzle angle can strip polymeric sand from joints, chip stone edges, or force water underneath pavers, creating problems that are worse than the stains you started with. If you’re only dealing with light buildup, our guide on<a href="/blog/how-to-clean-a-paver-patio/"> how to clean a paver patio</a> covers safe, simple upkeep you can do yourself.</p>
<h2><b>When Cleaning Won’t Fix It</b></h2>
<p>Sometimes a homeowner calls a cleaning company, gets the surface looking better temporarily, and the same staining or unevenness comes right back. That’s usually a sign the issue isn’t on the surface at all. A few patterns we see often:</p>
<ul><li>Recurring efflorescence in the same spot can mean water isn’t draining properly underneath the pavers.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Pavers that rock, sink, or separate point to a base that was never installed correctly or has shifted over time. Getting the<a href="/blog/paver-base-depth-for-patios-proper-installation-tips/"> base depth right</a> from the start is what prevents this in the first place.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Concrete that keeps cracking may be past the point where sealing or cleaning does much good.</li></ul>
<ul><li>A patio that’s clean but still doesn’t work for you, too small, poorly laid out, disconnected from the rest of the yard, isn’t something any amount of pressure washing will solve.</li></ul>
<p>In any of these cases, the better long-term investment is a redesign or new installation rather than repeated cleanings. This is the work we specialize in, whether that’s a fresh<a href="/services/paver-patios/"> paver patio installation</a> or addressing a driveway that’s ready for<a href="/blog/can-patio-pavers-be-used-for-a-driveway/"> a full paver upgrade</a>.</p>

<h2><b>Frequently Asked Questions</b></h2>
<h3><strong>How often should a driveway or patio be cleaned in Colorado? </strong></h3>
<p>It depends on shade, moisture exposure, and winter salt use, but most surfaces benefit from a cleaning at least once a year, often in spring after winter residue has built up. A local pressure washing company can advise on frequency for your specific surface.</p>
<h3><strong>Can I pressure wash my own paver patio? </strong></h3>
<p>You can for light maintenance, but it carries risk without the right equipment and pressure settings. Too much force can damage joint sand or chip natural stone.</p>
<h3><strong>What is that white powder on my pavers? </strong></h3>
<p>That’s likely efflorescence, a natural mineral deposit that rises to the surface as moisture evaporates. It’s common on natural stone. If it keeps coming back in the same area, it’s worth having the drainage and base checked rather than just cleaning it again.</p>
<h3><strong>Does salt from winter de-icing actually damage driveways? </strong></h3>
<p>Heavy, repeated salt exposure can contribute to surface wear and cracking over time, which is part of why some driveways eventually need more than a seasonal cleaning.</p>
<h3><strong>My patio is clean but still looks tired or doesn’t fit how we use the yard. What are my options? </strong></h3>
<p>That’s a design question, not a cleaning one. This is exactly where Ridgewell can help, whether that means a refreshed layout, new materials, or a full patio and driveway redesign.</p>

<h2><b>Thinking About More Than a Cleaning?</b></h2>
<p>If your patio or driveway keeps needing attention no matter how often it’s cleaned, or if it’s simply time for a layout and materials that actually fit how you use your space, Ridgewell Landscape and Design can help. We handle<a href="/services/"> hardscape design and installation</a> across the Denver area, including Broomfield, Parker, and nearby communities.<a href="/contact/"> Contact us</a> to talk through what’s going on and whether a redesign makes more sense than another round of cleaning.</p>

